# Southmeadow: the farming town in the far south, among the grain fields of the Sunfields and the orchards of the
# Orchard Vale, with a vineyard on the slopes; rich in grain and short of rain. The Apple Tree inn, the bakery, the
# smithy, the general store and its chalked prices, the chapel and its painted sun, the gate toll and the watch, the
# grain barn, the mill, the fields and the sky that won't rain.

scene rg_af_sky_watch
when topic=smalltalk|weather here.region=southmeadow here.weather=clear|overcast
a : Not a cloud since the new moon. | Clouds over the east ridge. Might rain. Might not. | I've looked at the sky so long my neck's stiff.
b[farmer] : Watching won't bring it. But you can't stop watching.
b[old] : The Sunfields were greener when my grandmother was young. She swore it.
b : The wheat's thirsty. | Don't hope too hard. | Everybody's has.
a : So am I. | I won't. | Everybody's.

scene rg_af_field_names
when topic=lore here.region=southmeadow
a : Old Sheaf can name every field in the Sunfields and who ploughed it last. | The Long Acre's been in the Harrow family a hundred years. | Every field here has a name. Some have two.
b[farmer] : Fields have names like wolves do. They've earned them.
b[old] : The Crooked Mile, the Widow's Strip, the Hare's Corner. I ploughed them all.
b : Remarkable memory. | And never sold. | Some have three.
a : Very. | Never. | Some do.

scene rg_af_first_sheaf
when topic=lore here.region=southmeadow here.season=autumn|summer
a : The first sheaf of the harvest goes to the chapel. | My mother keeps the first sheaf of every harvest in the rafters. Forty of them. | Who cuts the first sheaf this year?
b[priest] : The first sheaf for the Bright One, the rest for the hungry.
b[farmer] : The eldest on the farm cuts it. Always the eldest.
b : Old custom. | Forty! | The eldest, I'd guess.
a : Very old. | Dusty rafters. | Probably.

scene rg_af_grain_price
when topic=smalltalk here.region=southmeadow here.place=market|street|work
a : Grain price chalked by the store door went down again. | The city buyers want our grain for nothing. | Price is up a penny a sack. First good news in a month.
b[merchant] : The price is what the city pays. I just chalk it.
b[farmer] : We grow the city's bread and eat the crusts.
b : Again. | They always do. | Finally.
a : Again. | Always. | Finally.

scene rg_af_ford_toll
when topic=work|smalltalk here.region=southmeadow
a : Toll-keeper took a penny off every cart at the gate today. | Carts lined up at the gate all morning. | Somebody tried to come round through the orchards to dodge the toll. Got stuck in a ditch.
b[official] : The toll keeps the road mended. Mostly.
b[carter] : A penny to roll through a gate. Fair trade, I suppose.
b : Busy day. | Harvest carts. | Served them right.
a : Very busy. | Harvest. | Ha.

scene rg_af_amber_loaf
when topic=smalltalk here.region=southmeadow
a : Best bread in the south at the bakery. | The bakery ran out before noon. | Sunfields grain makes the sweetest bread there is.
b[baker] : Our own grain, our own mill, our own oven. Can't beat that.
b : Agreed. | Again? Go earlier. | It does.
a : Still warm, too. | I'll try. | And the crust!

scene rg_af_brewhouse
when topic=smalltalk here.region=southmeadow
a : You can smell the brewhouse mash across the square. | New barley ale at the Apple Tree tonight. | The brewer bought half the barley harvest.
b[brewer] : Our barley makes ale golden as the fields.
b : Smells good. | I'll be there. | Thirsty town.
a : Very good. | See you there. | Very thirsty.

scene rg_af_lost_land
when topic=lore here.region=southmeadow pair.band=friends|family|acquaintances
a : The Furrows lost their land after the dry year. Work another's now. | One bad harvest and a family's gone from the fields. | My neighbour's saving to buy a field. Third year now.
b[farmer] : The land gives and the land takes. Mostly it takes.
b : Hard thing. | Too true. | Good for them.
a : Very hard. | Too true. | Very good.

scene rg_af_harvest_chapel
when topic=lore here.region=southmeadow
a : Somebody's repainting the sun on the chapel ceiling. | Sheaves stacked on the altar after the harvest. | They pray for rain in the chapel every week now.
b[priest] : The Bright One sends what we need. Not always what we want.
b : Bright gold. | Every year. | Every day, soon.
a : Very bright. | Every year. | Soon.

scene rg_af_mill
when topic=work here.region=southmeadow a.job=farmer|laborer|baker
a : The mill's been grinding day and night since harvest. | The millstream's too low to turn the wheel. | Waited all day at the mill for my sacks.
b[laborer] : No water, no wheel. No wheel, no flour.
b : Good harvest, then. | Dry year. | Busy miller.
a : Good enough. | Very dry. | Very busy.

scene rg_af_grain_barn
when topic=lore|work here.region=southmeadow
a : The grain barn's stacked to the beams. Good year. | Rats in the grain barn again. Somebody get a cat. | The grain barn's half empty. The dry year hit hard.
b[farmer] : A full barn and a quiet winter. That's all I ask.
b : Thank the sun. | Two cats. | Hard winter ahead.
a : Thank it. | Three. | Very hard.

scene rg_af_ford_forge
when topic=work here.region=southmeadow a.job=smith|apprentice|farmer
a : Sharpened forty sickles before harvest. | The smithy's booked till the last sheaf. | Broke my scythe on a stone in the Long Acre.
b[smith] : Sharpen before harvest, not during. Nobody listens.
b : Busy forge. | Every year. | Bad luck.
a : Very busy. | Every year. | Very bad.

scene rg_af_dust_storm
when topic=weather|lore here.region=southmeadow here.weather=sandstorm|storm|overcast
a : Dust storm took the topsoil off the east fields. | Grit in my teeth, grit in my bread. | The sky went yellow at noon.
b[old] : Dust storms come when the fields are thirsty. They've been thirsty a long while.
b : Bad sign. | Every storm. | Strange light.
a : Very bad. | Every one. | Very strange.

scene rg_af_harvest_feast
when topic=lore here.region=southmeadow here.season=autumn
a : Harvest home at the Apple Tree tonight. Whole town's going. | Harvest garlands over the bar at the Apple Tree. | They dance round the last stook at harvest home.
b[innkeeper] : Ale on the house for the last cart in. Old custom.
b : Wouldn't miss it. | Lovely. | Every year.
a : Nor would I. | Very lovely. | Every year.

scene rg_af_steppe_wide
when topic=smalltalk here.region=southmeadow
a : The Sunfields go on to the edge of the sky. | You can see a cart coming half a day off. | No hills out there. Just grain and sky.
b[adolescent] : I'd like to see a mountain someday. Just once.
b : They do. | Nothing to hide behind. | Gold and blue.
a : Forever. | Nothing. | Gold and blue.

scene rg_af_rain_dance
when topic=lore here.region=southmeadow pair.band=friends|family|acquaintances
a : My grandmother used to spill a cup of water on the fields every dawn. For luck. | The old folk say singing brings rain. Nobody sings well enough. | Somebody poured ale on the Long Acre to bring rain.
b[priest] : Prayer brings comfort. Rain comes when it comes.
b : Did it work? | Ha. | Waste of ale.
a : Sometimes. | Nobody. | A terrible waste.

scene rg_af_toll_and_watch
when topic=work here.region=southmeadow a.role=guard
a : The toll and the watch share one gatehouse. Cramped as a hen coop. | Took the toll and broke up a fight before noon. | Two of us for a town and all those fields. Never enough.
b[guard] : Toll in the morning, watch at night. No rest.
b : Cosy. | Busy morning. | Never.
a : Very cosy. | Very busy. | Never enough.

scene rg_af_grain_carts
when topic=work here.region=southmeadow a.job=carter|farmer|merchant
a : Grain carts heading north for the city at dawn. | Carted forty sacks to the mill. | A grain cart overturned in the ditch. Half the sacks wet.
b[carter] : Grain's heavy and the roads are long. Every cart's a week of my life.
b : Long road. | Heavy work. | Bad luck.
a : Very long. | Very heavy. | Very bad.

scene rg_af_locusts
when topic=lore here.region=southmeadow
a : My grandfather saw the locust year. The sky went black. | They say the locusts come every forty years. It's been thirty-nine. | Saw a grasshopper this morning and nearly fainted.
b[old] : I saw it. Every green thing gone in a day. Every one.
b[farmer] : Don't say it out loud. Don't.
b : Terrible. | Don't count. | It's just a grasshopper.
a : Terrible. | I can't help it. | So far.

scene rg_af_ford_house
when topic=smalltalk here.region=southmeadow here.place=tavern
a : Grain dust in every crack of the Apple Tree. | The toll-keeper's table by the window's always taken. | Farm carts woke me at dawn at the Apple Tree.
b[innkeeper] : The dust is the harvest. The harvest pays my bills.
b : In my fur too. | Somebody should move him. | Every dawn.
a : Mine too. | Not me. | Every single one.

scene rg_af_sun_field
when topic=lore here.region=southmeadow
a : The south slope always ripens first. Nobody knows why. | They say the south slope was blessed by a priest long ago. | The south slope's the best ground in the Sunfields.
b[farmer] : South slope, deep soil. Not a blessing. Just good ground.
b : Strange. | So they say. | It is.
a : Very strange. | So they say. | The best.

scene rg_af_owls
when topic=smalltalk here.region=southmeadow here.time=dusk|night
a : Barn owls hunting over the stubble at dusk. | An owl lives in the grain barn rafters. Keeps the rats down. | Heard the owl call three times. Bad luck, they say.
b[farmer] : An owl's worth three cats. Leave it be.
b : Lovely sight. | Good owl. | Old nonsense.
a : Very lovely. | Very good. | Probably.

scene rg_af_wells
when topic=lore|work here.region=southmeadow here.season=summer
a : The town well's lower than I've ever seen. | Digging the well deeper. Hit rock. | They're rationing water from the well.
b[old] : The well went dry once, when I was young. We drank from the millstream.
b : Dry year. | Bad luck. | It's come to that.
a : Very dry. | Very bad. | It has.

scene rg_af_seed
when topic=work|smalltalk here.region=southmeadow here.season=spring
a : Sowing the long field. Hope the rain comes. | Seed's dear this spring. Lost half to mice. | The Sunfields are green for one week in spring. Look at them.
b[farmer] : Sow in hope, reap in sweat. That's farming.
b : So do I. | Cursed mice. | Beautiful.
a : So do I. | Cursed. | They are.

scene rg_af_young_leaving
when topic=smalltalk here.region=southmeadow pair.band=friends|family|spouses
a : My eldest wants to leave for Ridgemere. | Every spring another young one goes off to the city. | Farm's too small for three pups to inherit.
b[old] : They go, they come back, they go again. The fields pull.
b : Let them go. | Every spring. | Too true.
a : Maybe. | Every spring. | Too true.

scene rg_af_scarecrows
when topic=lore|smalltalk here.region=southmeadow
a : The Harrows dressed their scarecrow in the old toll-keeper's coat. | Every field has a scarecrow with a name. | Crows sat on the scarecrow's head. Not frightened at all.
b[farmer] : A scarecrow's only as good as the wolf who moves it.
b : He'd laugh. | What's yours called? | Clever crows.
a : He would. | Old Straw. | Too clever.

scene rg_af_stubble_fires
when topic=lore|work here.region=southmeadow here.season=autumn
a : Burning the stubble on the east fields. | Smoke over the Sunfields from every farm. | The stubble fire jumped the ditch last year.
b[old] : Burn the stubble, sweeten the ground. Watch the wind.
b : Smoky week. | Every autumn. | Watch the wind.
a : Very smoky. | Every autumn. | I will.

scene rg_af_toll_keeper
when topic=smalltalk here.region=southmeadow
a : The toll-keeper knows every cart by its creak. | The toll-keeper's never missed a day in thirty years. | The toll-keeper let a widow's cart through free.
b[official] : A good toll-keeper knows when the rule bends.
b : Sharp ears. | Never ill? | Kind wolf.
a : Very sharp. | Never. | Very kind.

scene rg_sm_orchard_vale
when topic=work|smalltalk here.region=southmeadow here.season=summer|autumn
a : Apple picking in the Orchard Vale starts tomorrow. Every ladder's spoken for. | Wasps in the windfalls. Stung twice before noon. | The pear trees in the Orchard Vale are breaking under the fruit.
b[farmer] : Pick by hand, never shake. A bruised apple's a cider apple.
b[child] : I ate six apples off the ground! My belly hurts!
b : I'll bring mine. | Leave the windfalls. | Prop the branches.
a : Bring two. | I will now. | Already did.

scene rg_sm_vineyard
when topic=work|lore here.region=southmeadow here.season=summer|autumn
a : The grapes on the vineyard slope are sweet this year. | The vineyard wants dry weather and for once it's getting it. | Treading grapes all week. My paws are purple.
b[farmer] : Dry summer, good wine. The only thing a dry summer's good for.
b[old] : Best wine I ever drank came off that slope. Forty years ago.
b : Good year, then. | About time. | They'll wash.
a : A good one. | About time. | Eventually.
